From 78a13508f6a49d9d1741edd3e55d5cade3b84fa4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mew <phroun@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 23:37:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] parser: OSC 52 clipboard, and fix ST terminating an OSC one
 byte early

Two things, because the second blocks the first.

ST. handleOSCString treated a bare ESC as the terminator and returned to
ground WITHOUT consuming the '\' of a real ST, so the backslash was then
processed from ground state and printed. Every ST-terminated OSC did
this. It has gone unnoticed because the 7000-series are BEL-terminated in
practice -- but OSC 52 traffic from real programs is overwhelmingly
ST-terminated, so it would have surfaced the day OSC 52 landed. Fixed
with a one-state detour (stateOSCEsc): on ESC inside an OSC string,
expect one more byte; '\' executes, anything else abandons the OSC and
reprocesses the byte as the escape sequence it actually began.

OSC 52. The clipboard escape sequence -- the one channel a program
running INSIDE a terminal has to the system clipboard, and what vim's
"+y, tmux's set-clipboard, neovim, emacs and zellij all emit. Parsed and
policy-checked at the shared layer, then handed to a callback the front
end registers, because only the front end knows what the clipboard is on
its platform (GTK's clipboard, Qt's QClipboard, a host's desktop, or --
for a standalone PurfecTerm inside kitty or tmux -- re-emitting it
outward so nested stacks work).

  purfecterm.ClipboardEvent / ClipboardPolicy / DefaultClipboardPolicy
  (*Parser).SetOnClipboard, SetClipboardPolicy, SetResponseSink
  (*cli.Terminal).SetOnClipboard, SetClipboardPolicy, SendResponse
  cli.Options.ClipboardWrite / ClipboardRead / ClipboardLimit

Defaults follow xterm and kitty, and the two directions differ on
purpose. WRITES act (default on): initiated by the user's own program,
worst case is clipboard spam from something they chose to run. READS are
denied (default off): a query lets any program that can print to the
terminal -- a cat'ed file, a script's output -- silently exfiltrate
whatever is on the clipboard, which is regularly a password. A denied
query is answered with an EMPTY payload rather than silence, because
programs block waiting for the reply. Payloads are capped (1 MiB
default) and an oversized or non-base64 payload CLEARS rather than
truncating: half a secret on the clipboard is worse than none.

With no callback registered OSC 52 is consumed and dropped -- today's
behaviour, and the right default for a consumer that has not opted in.

SetResponseSink is the channel the terminal answers on. DSR and DA are
stubbed in the CSI handler ("would need to send response - ignore") and
want exactly this sink; that is the obvious next use.

Not touched: the GTK and Qt widgets, which need their own three-line
bridge from the callback to the platform clipboard. The shared layer is
done and the cli adapter is wired.
